:''This article summarizes all the elections in the U.S. House of Representatives in the November 2010 election cycle. For complete list of the races in all districts without any commentary, see [[United States House of Representatives elections, 2010 - complete list]].''

'''The 2010 United States House of Representatives elections''' will be held on November 2, 2010, halfway through [[President of the United States|President]] [[Barack Obama]]'s term in office. Elections will be held for all 435 seats, representing the 50 [[U.S. state]]s. Elections also will be held for the [[Delegate (United States Congress)|delegates]] from the [[District of Columbia]] and four of the five major [[Insular area|U.S. territories]]. The only seat in the [[United States House of Representatives]] not up for election is that of the [[Resident Commissioner of Puerto Rico]], who serves a four-year term and will next face election in 2012.

The winners of this election cycle will serve in the [[112th United States Congress|112th Congress]].  This is expected to be the last congressional election using congressional districts apportioned based on the [[2000 United States Census]].

==Background==
Following the [[United States House of Representatives elections, 2006|2006 elections]], Democrats took control of the House as well as the Senate. In the [[United States House of Representatives elections, 2008|2008 elections]], which coincided with Democrat [[Barack Obama]]'s [[United States presidential election, 2008|victory]] over Republican [[John McCain]] for the [[President of the United States|presidency]], Democrats increased their majorities in both chambers. Of the 435 congressional districts, 242 were carried by Obama, while 193 voted for McCain. Of the districts Obama won, 34 elected a Republican to the House, while 49 of the districts McCain won elected a Democrat.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://innovation.cq.com/atlas/district_08?referrer=tout|title=CQ Politics &#124; Presidential and House election 08 results by congressional district|publisher=Innovation.cq.com|date=|accessdate=2009-07-28}}</ref>

On September 23, 2010, Republican members of the House of Representatives presented a [[Pledge to America]], designed to imitate the 1994 [[Contract with America]] and highlighting the proposed Republican agenda in the event of getting back in power after only two years of controlling either no chamber nor the White House.

==Retiring incumbents==
37 [[U.S. Representative]]s will retire at the end of their current term and 5 vacancies will be filled by special elections before November.

