==Predictions==
The following are predictions of the outcome of the 2010 United States House of Representatives elections.
===Overview===
* [[RealClearPolitics]].
**As of October 25, 2010, RCP projects the Republicans will take 222 seats, the Democrats will take 180, and 33 races are toss-ups.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.realclearpolitics.com/elections/ |title=RCP 2010 Elections |publisher=Realclearpolitics.com |date= |accessdate=2010-09-26}}</ref>

* [[Nate Silver]], FiveThirtyEight (New York Times)
**As of October 8, 2010, Nate Silver's prediction model projects the Republicans will win (on average) 226.5 seats, and the Democrats will win 208.5. The Republicans have a 72% chance of winning the majority.<ref>{{cite web|last=Silver |first=Nate |url=http://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/10/08/projected-republican-gains-approach-50-house-seats// |title=Projected Republican Gains Approach 50 House Seats- NYTimes.com |publisher=Fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com |date=2010-10-8 |accessdate=2010-10-9}}</ref>

* [[Sabato's Crystal Ball|Crystal Ball]]
**In a September 2010 column for his newsletter, [[Larry Sabato]] wrote "Given what we can see at this moment, Republicans have a good chance to win the House by picking up as many as 47 seats, net."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/ljs2010090201/ |title=Larry J. Sabato's Crystal Ball » SIXTY DAYS TO GO |publisher=Centerforpolitics.org |date=2010-09-02 |accessdate=2010-09-26}}</ref>

* [[Charlie Cook]]
**On October 26, 2010, The Cook Political Report raised its House forecast to "a Democratic net loss of 48 to 60 seats, with higher losses possible."<ref>{{cite web|author=Tuesday, October 26, 2010 |url=http://hotlineoncall.nationaljournal.com/archives/2010/10/cook-political-8.php |title=Cook Political Ups Projected Democratic Losses To 48 to 60 |date=2010-10-26}}</ref>
**In a February 2010 interview with ''[[National Journal]]'', he said that "it's very hard to come up with a scenario where Democrats don't lose the House. It's very hard."<ref>{{cite web|author=Monday, February 22, 2010 |url=http://www.outsidethebeltway.com/archives/democrats_will_lose_the_house_charlie_cook/ |title=Democrats Will Lose the House: Charlie Cook |publisher=Outsidethebeltway.com |date=2010-02-22 |accessdate=2010-06-14}}</ref>

* [[Congressional Quarterly]].
**In July 2010, Congressional Quarterly projects the Democrats will take 205 seats, the Republicans 190, and they consider 40 races too close to call.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://innovation.cqpolitics.com/atlas/house2010_rr |title=CQ Politics &#124; House Race Ratings Map for 2010 |publisher=Innovation.cqpolitics.com |date= |accessdate=2010-07-11}}</ref>

* [[Rothenberg Political Report]].
**In April 2010, [[Stuart Rothenberg]] wrote on his [[blog]] that "... the atmospherics remain strongly behind the GOP, and major Republican House gains are extremely likely" and that "it's clear that the battleground is almost entirely on Democratic soil. Obviously, control of the House is at risk."<ref>{{cite web|last=Rothenberg |first=The |url=http://rothenbergpoliticalreport.blogspot.com/2010/04/new-print-edition-house-overview.html |title=The Rothenberg Political Report: New Print Edition: House Overview |publisher=Rothenbergpoliticalreport.blogspot.com |date=2010-04-14 |accessdate=2010-06-14}}</ref>

